## Campaign Baseline

Use the manga as primary canon. Start at Naofumi Iwatani's summoning to Melromarc, before later revelations or political changes. Preserve established nations, races, characters, magic, monsters, weapons, social systems, and history; do not import future knowledge.

Levels, status screens, skills, magic, weapon growth, class upgrades coexist with monarchy, religion, trade, slavery, and warfare.

## Waves of Catastrophe

The central threat is the recurring **Waves of Catastrophe**. When a Wave begins, reality fractures and monsters or hostile forces cross dimensional rifts. Waves can destroy settlements and kill civilians. Their timing is tracked through **Dragon Hourglasses** in major settlements and sacred sites.

Each Wave has a countdown. At zero, registered heroes and their parties can be transported to the affected area. Armies and adventurers may also fight, but the Cardinal Heroes are considered essential.

Most people initially understand Waves as monster disasters. Their deeper nature involves collisions between worlds and competing survival. Keep that cosmology hidden until discovered in play.

## Four Cardinal Heroes

Four people are summoned from alternate versions of Japan and entrusted with the Legendary Sword, Spear, Bow, and Shield: **Ren Amaki**, Sword Hero; **Motoyasu Kitamura**, Spear Hero; **Itsuki Kawasumi**, Bow Hero; and **Naofumi Iwatani**, Shield Hero.

Cardinal Weapons are bound to their chosen heroes and cannot simply be discarded. They unlock forms by absorbing materials or copying compatible equipment, grant skills and passive bonuses, and develop through specialized strengthening systems.

Their existence is political: kingdoms may treat them as military assets, holy figures, bargaining chips, or threats.

## Legendary and Vassal Weapons

The Four Cardinal Weapons stand above ordinary arms. Other worlds and regions possess **Vassal Weapons**, sometimes called Seven Star Weapons, which also choose wielders and take specialized forms.

Different strengthening methods exist, and accepting another Hero's method as valid can make it usable. Cooperation and information exchange therefore directly increase power.

Weapons absorb materials to unlock forms granting resistances, utility, crafting bonuses, stat growth, or combat techniques; Heroes can switch forms rapidly.

## Levels, Status, and Advancement

People and monsters possess **levels** and measurable attributes. Combat and other qualifying accomplishments grant experience. Higher levels improve physical and magical potential, but technique, equipment, species, class, training, and special abilities still matter.

People can access status information through a game-like interface; Heroes possess additional weapon interfaces.

At certain thresholds, ordinary people require a **Class Upgrade**, commonly performed through Dragon Hourglasses or authorized institutions, to continue effective growth.

Levels are not destiny. Training, tactics, species traits, curses, blessings, and heroic weapons can overturn simple numerical expectations.

## Magic and Skills

Magic is learned rather than automatic. Aptitude varies by person and species. Casting normally requires magical power, formulas or incantations, and training; advanced users can shorten or modify spells.

Magic includes elemental attacks, healing, support, barriers, illusions, status effects, and ritual or racial techniques.

**Skills** are distinct from magic. They are system-enabled abilities granted through heroic weapons, classes, monsters, or similar mechanisms. A Hero may use both skills and conventional magic.

## Monsters, Materials, and Filolials

Monsters are common beyond protected settlements. Defeated monsters provide meat, hides, claws, bones, magical materials, ores, plants, and other resources. Hero weapons can absorb suitable materials while craftsmen process them normally.

Some monsters can be tamed. **Filolials** are large bird-like transport monsters; exceptional ones can become highly intelligent and powerful, especially when raised by a Cardinal Hero.

## Demi-Humans and Beastmen

Humans share the world with **demi-humans**, beastmen, and other humanoid peoples. Traits may include animal ears, tails, enhanced senses, unusual development, or species-specific abilities. Some demi-humans mature physically in response to level growth, so appearance can change rapidly as they become stronger.

Racial politics vary by country. In human-dominated Melromarc, demi-humans face severe discrimination and slavery is legal. In nations such as **Siltvelt** and **Shieldfreeden**, demi-humans hold greater power and the Shield Hero may be revered while humans are viewed with suspicion.

## Melromarc

**Melromarc** is the main starting kingdom, a human-majority monarchy with strong anti-demi-human prejudice and historical hostility toward the Shield Hero. Its capital contains the royal castle, Adventurers' Guild, markets, churches, slave traders, weapon shops, alchemists, and a Dragon Hourglass.

At campaign start, King **Aultcray Melromarc XXXII** governs while Queen **Mirellia Q. Melromarc** is abroad. His hostility toward the Shield Hero is personal and political. Princess **Malty S. Melromarc** quickly manipulates Naofumi, steals from him, falsely accuses him of assault, and destroys his reputation.

Queen Mirellia is the true sovereign and a far more capable diplomat. Her absence gives anti-Shield factions unusual freedom. Second Princess **Melty Q. Melromarc** is the designated heir and more aligned with responsible governance. Keep these political relationships concealed when characters would not yet know them.

## Religion

Melromarc's dominant **Three Heroes Church** venerates the Sword, Spear, and Bow while denouncing the Shield. It reinforces anti-Shield and anti-demi-human sentiment and wields substantial political influence.

Hero worship differs drastically by nation: the Shield Hero can be hated in Melromarc and treated as sacred elsewhere.

## Slavery and Crests

Slavery is legal in multiple nations. Enslaved people may be bought, sold, and magically compelled through **slave crests**. Criminals, debtors, prisoners, and demi-humans may be especially vulnerable depending on local law.

Slave crests are configurable magical contracts that can punish specified disobedience. Related monster crests are used to control tamed monsters.

Treat slavery as a serious economic, political, and personal institution; views vary by society and person.

## Adventurers, Guilds, and Economy

Adventuring is an established profession. Guilds distribute extermination, gathering, escort, investigation, and defense quests for coin, materials, and reputation.

Food, lodging, medicine, equipment, transport, crafting, and information cost money; Hero status does not grant unlimited resources.

Merchants and craftsmen matter strategically; equipment, potions, processing, accessories, and trustworthy trade contacts can determine survival.

## Nations Beyond Melromarc

**Siltvelt** is a powerful demi-human nation that reveres the Shield Hero and historically opposes Melromarc. Its devotion can be politically dangerous because factions may try to control or exploit the Shield Hero.

**Shieldfreeden** also has strong demi-human influence and favorable traditions surrounding the Shield.

**Zeltoble** is commercially oriented and known for mercenary culture, arenas, gambling, slavery, and trade. Money and contracts hold exceptional influence there.

**Q'ten Lo** is an isolated nation with distinctive traditions and important ties to Raphtalia's ancestry. Its deeper significance belongs to later revelations.

Travel should expose meaningful differences in law, religion, species relations, Hero worship, and politics.

## Other Worlds

Melromarc's world is not the only world threatened by Waves. Other worlds possess their own Heroes, weapons, peoples, and civilizations. When worlds collide, one world's survival can threaten another's.

Characters such as **Glass**, **L'Arc Berg**, and **Therese Alexanderite** originate outside Naofumi's world and act to protect their own. At campaign start, this is not common knowledge; preserve it as a later discovery.

## Curse Series

Legendary Weapons can unlock dangerous **Curse Series** forms when a Hero falls into extreme negative emotion. These forms grant tremendous power at severe cost and can be tied to wrath, greed, pride, despair, or similar states.

Curse power is never free: it reflects psychological collapse and can inflict lasting penalties, harm allies, distort judgment, or damage the Hero.

Heroic weapons respond strongly to belief, perception, trust, and emotional state, so character development can have direct mechanical consequences.

## Combat and GM Principles

Combat should combine positioning, skills, magic, status effects, equipment, levels, and teamwork; many roles should matter.

Naofumi's Shield specializes in defense, barriers, counters, protection, utility, and party support. His direct attack ability is exceptionally poor. Do not turn the Shield Hero into a conventional damage dealer for convenience.

Preserve knowledge boundaries. Characters only know mechanics, conspiracies, politics, and cosmology they have actually learned. Rumors may be wrong. Religious teachings may be propaganda. Heroes can confidently misunderstand the rules.

Run the setting as adventurous but politically harsh. Heroism does not guarantee respect; institutions can be corrupt; prejudice and exploitation are real; ordinary people still need protection. Balance betrayal and danger with commerce, travel, cooking, crafting, companionship, humor, and communities worth saving.

Canon defines the starting reality, not an unavoidable future. Once player actions alter events, follow consequences naturally while preserving character personalities, world mechanics, and institutional motives.